{"records":[{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tkh39ck93y","title":"A genome-wide screen identifies silencers with distinct chromatin properties and mechanisms of repression. Hofbauer et al.","description":"Cell type-specific gene transcription is a profoundly regulated process that enables development and homeostasis in all animals. Two major classes of distal cis-regulatory DNA elements (CREs), enhancers and silencers, activate or repress transcription, respectively. However, while enhancers have been thoroughly characterized, the sequence and chromatin properties of silencers, as well as their mechanisms of distal transcriptional repression, remain largely unknown. We performed an unbiased genome-wide silencer screen in Drosophila melanogaster S2 cells and discover a novel class of silencers, which recruit one of three different zinc finger transcription factors (TFs) through conserved DNA motifs. Surprisingly, these silencers lack detectable DNA accessibility, placing them outside current chromatin-defined catalogs of CREs (e.g. modENCODE). Further we find that the TF CG11247, which we term Saft, binds to one type of silencers via the orphan DLM3 motif and seems to safeguard cell fate decisions by repressing trachea- and cuticle-specific genes in non- trachea/non-cuticle tissues, such as the ovary or brain. Saft contains the previously unknown, highly conserved effector domain ZAC and functions via the corepressor G9a, yet independently of G9a’s H3K9 methyltransferase activity. Overall, our identification of silencers with unexpected properties and mechanism of action has important implications for the understanding and future study of repressive CREs, as well as the functional annotation of animal genomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-12T10:07:33.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tkh39ck93y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tkh39ck93y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorena","last_name":"Hofbauer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Stark","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013tf3c58","name":"FWF Austrian Science Fund","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/00q32j219","name":"Boehringer Ingelheim (Germany)","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/028jc0449","name":"Austrian Research Promotion Agency","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/03tx8dq56","name":"Boehringer Ingelheim Foundation","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Genomics","Transcription","Gene Expression"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vg3r5355bj","title":"Code used for \"  An evolutionary model identifies the main selective pressures for the evolution of genome-replication profiles.\" ","description":"Code used for \"  An evolutionary model identifies the main selective pressures for the evolution of genome-replication profiles.\"\n\nAt each cell cycle, a yeast cell replicates its genome by a prescribed but stochastic temporal program encoded by the localization and strength of a set of discrete \"replication origins\" along the genome. This temporal program is of primary importance for cell viability and, consequently, on evolutionary time scale it is subject to selective pressures. By mathematical modelling of replication-origins evolution this study identifies the two main selective pressures responsible for evolutionary appearance and disappearance of replication origins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-13T21:38:07.95Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vg3r5355bj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vg3r5355bj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"rossana","last_name":"droghetti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computer Simulation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w8jtmnszd9","title":"The Proteomic Landscape of Genome-Wide Genetic Perturbations","description":"Functional genomic strategies have become fundamental for annotating gene function and regulatory networks. Here, we combined functional genomics with proteomics by quantifying protein abundances in a genome-scale knock-out library in Saccharomyces cerevisiae, using data-independent acquisition mass spectrometry. We thus created a large, systematic, and quantitative proteomic dataset, with an average of 2,520 proteins quantified across 4,699 yeast gene knock-out strains. The proteome profiles comprise over 100 million peptide quantitations and 9 million protein quantitations. These link deleted genes to proteins and provide a genome-scale resource of molecular phenotypes for 79% of the coding yeast genome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-22T06:58:00.663Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w8jtmnszd9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w8jtmnszd9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christoph","last_name":"Messner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Georg","last_name":"Kustatscher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Ralser","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Systems Biology","Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wkttx6tp2d","title":"S. aureus transposon sequencing in the murine vaginal tract ","description":"Results from TRANSIT Tn-seq conditional essentiality analysis, associated to the paper \"Genome-wide mutagenesis identifies factors involved in MRSA vaginal colonization \" by Lyon et al. (2025), under review at Cell Reports. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-18T22:39:47.454Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wkttx6tp2d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wkttx6tp2d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Laurie","last_name":"Lyon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transposon","Vagina","Mouse Model","Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":138982,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":162,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":138982,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138296,"restricted":67,"embargoed":619},"top_f